"""Diagnostics for the MIB compiler: the `Diagnostic` record, the code catalogue and a collector.

Levels follow libsmi/smilint, so `smilint -l N` output can be compared line by line:

    0  internal error (a bug in the compiler, or an unknown diagnostic code)
    1  data lost: a declaration or clause was dropped
    2  RFC violation; the meaning was recovered or guessed
    3  minor RFC violation
    4  questionable but legal
    5  style
    6  info

Levels 1-2 are errors, 3-4 warnings and 5-6 info.

Every diagnostic has a stable string code such as `lex.unterminated-string` or
`parse.missing-comma`. The prefix names the stage that reports it (`lex.`, `parse.`,
`source.`; later stages add their own). Tests, `--ignore` style options and corpus summaries
match on codes, never on message text. `CATALOG` maps each code to its default level, the
closest libsmi error name (or "" when libsmi has none) and a one-line description.
"""

LEVEL_INTERNAL = 0
LEVEL_ERROR = 1
LEVEL_RFC = 2
LEVEL_MINOR = 3
LEVEL_WARNING = 4
LEVEL_STYLE = 5
LEVEL_INFO = 6

# code -> (default level, libsmi error name or "", description)
CATALOG: dict[str, (int, str, str)] = {
    "internal": (0, "", "internal compiler error or unknown diagnostic code"),
    # --- lexical (mib.lexer, and string decoding in mib.parser) ---------------------------
    "lex.unterminated-string": (2, "", "a quoted string is not closed before the end of the file; it is cut at the end of its first line and lexing resumes there"),
    "lex.escaped-quote": (2, "", "a doubled quote inside a string (the X.680 escape) is read as one quote character"),
    "lex.string-stray-quote": (2, "", "a stray quote inside a string made the following code a string; the string ends at the next quote instead"),
    "lex.string-missing-quote": (2, "", "a string is not closed before the code that follows it; it is cut at the end of its text"),
    "parse.string-embedded-quote": (2, "", "unescaped quotes inside a string: the pieces are joined into one string"),
